[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`nlit` can become huge.** Writing to `lens[361..364)` sets `nlit`'s upper
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes, so `nlit` can reach ~10^6. The final `cp_build(s, s->lit, lens, nlit)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   then walks megabytes off the end of both the stack array and `s->lit`, and both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   libraries take SIGSEGV.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/lib.rs` therefore models the frame byte-for-byte (`cp_dyn_frame`, with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compile-time `offset_of!` assertions) and routes every `lens[]` access through a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bounds check that faults (`cp_lost_control`) where the C would be scribbling
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m arbitrarily far up its stack. `tests/t06_lens_overflow.rs` drives the overflow
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deliberately (rows C38a–C38e in `CONFIGS.md`) instead of relying on fuzzing to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stumble into it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## G. Known-divergent-by-nature: nothing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No remaining behaviour is left unmatched. The two constructs that looked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unmatchable at first (`lens[-1]` and the `lens[]` overflow) turned out to be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deterministic at `-O0` and are modelled; see section F.
